WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.129
The underfunding of

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.018
schools in Los Angeles,

00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:05.338
California

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:06.179
South Mississippi.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:06.319
Minnesota,

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.099
North Carolina,

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:08.669
Missouri,

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:09.839
Washington, D.C.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.129
The underfunding of schools in New

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:12.599
Mexico is devastating to our

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:13.469
children.

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:14.999
I don't know about you, but when I

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.859
hear the phrase "the underfunding of

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.019
school," my head and my wallet

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:19.859
explode.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.599
Tax revenue per student in public

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.849
K-12 schools is up 24

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.499
percent nationally over the past two

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.509
decades, and that takes inflation

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:28.709
into account.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.689
In New York, where I live, real

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.669
per pupil revenue has increased

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.499
by a mind boggling 68

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.449
percent over that same period.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:37.919
The public schools here are now

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.319
shelling out more than $30'000

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.149
per kid, or more than double

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.529
the national average.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.419
And that doesn't even include

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.369
the $16 billion extra that

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:49.109
New York's system got in federal and

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.479
state funding for COVID 19 relief.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:52.859
Yet New York's public schools are

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.749
still as terrible as the Mets, the

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.819
Jets and the Giants, with only

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.769
a third or fewer of students up

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:00.509
to grade level in eighth grade

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.369
reading and math, according to

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.199
their scores on a nationwide

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.789
test, that's the gold standard for

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:07.199
measuring performance.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.029
30 grand a year puts the

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:10.859
lie to the argument pushed by

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.509
unions and progressive advocates

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.579
that more money will fix schools.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.169
More money hasn't helped the rest of

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.549
the country boost their scores,

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.829
either. None of this is a mystery.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.059
The connection between bigger

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.009
spending and better outcomes is weak

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:24.899
at best and nonexistent at

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.969
worst. Certainly, the new money

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:28.499
in New York hasn't gone to

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.329
fundamentally reform what

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:32.219
gets taught or how or under

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.629
what circumstances.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.069
According to a report by my

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:36.929
colleagues at the Reason Foundation,

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.819
teacher compensation is way up in

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.859
New York, especially when it comes

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.449
to health insurance and pensions,

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.669
which have grown by 147

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.679
percent. Nationwide, a dozen

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.509
states increase spending on teachers

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:51.059
benefits by over 100 percent,

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.709
and only three states kept the

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.419
increase below 10 percent.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:55.439
Costs for things like

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.119
administration, support staff and

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.309
transportation are up 24

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:00.089
percent.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.129
Dumping more money into a broken

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.989
system is like trying

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.969
to fix a leaky pipe by pouring more

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:06.899
water into it.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.269
What needs to happen is a revolution

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.159
in how education is conceived

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:11.999
and delivered.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.919
Over the past 20 years, New York has

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.449
allowed publicly funded charter

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.219
schools, which must attract and keep

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.629
students in order to stay in

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.489
business. The best charters have

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:22.169
waitlists, even though they get less

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:23.399
money than traditional public

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.499
schools. But like most places,

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:26.969
New York caps the number of

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:28.859
charters, and it doesn't even allow

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:30.809
education savings accounts,

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.179
tax credit scholarships and vouchers

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.769
that would allow more families to

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:36.779
escape traditional public schools.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.669
Increasing the amount and variety of

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.559
school choice, though, is exactly

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.689
what New York and the country need

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:43.829
to be doing.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.539
We're not going to seriously improve

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.549
educational outcomes for our kids

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:49.649
if we don't fundamentally change how

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:50.969
we educate them.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.559
When you look back 20 years,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.419
virtually every other service in

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:56.339
our lives, from coffee drinks,

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.169
to media to medicine, has gone

00:02:58.170 --> 00:02:59.969
through multiple revolutions.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:01.529
Everything is more geared toward the

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:02.429
individual.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.349
It's more responsive and not just

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.599
cheaper in real terms, but better

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.519
too. K-12 education

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.229
is still basically the same as it

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.089
was when today's parents were

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.039
in school. The only difference is

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.719
the price tag, which just keeps

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:16.979
going up and up.

